{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1774 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "17d8b4f1d35e7efac62c392ac38e1ca068dd7cfbcc4c8d1bf29f8c311218bce1" :edb/db-before-t 1773 :edb/db-after-t 1774 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535086 50 "2026-10-13T00:34:35.781Z" 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186054489 1071 "Find the $\\textrm{GCF}$ of $8a$, $4c$ and $12c$." 13194139535086 17592186045417 false] [17592186054489 1071 "Find the $\\textrm{GCF}$ of $9a$, $18a,$ and $27a.$" 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186054489 1074 "First, we decompose each expression into its separate factors. We express each constant as a product of its prime factors, and we treat each variable in the product as a separate factor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8a &= 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot a \\\\\n4c &= 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot c \\\\\n12c &= 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now match up the individual factors as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8a &= 2_(―) \\cdot 2_(―) \\cdot 2 \\cdot a \\\\\n4c &= 2_(―) \\cdot 2_(―) \\cdot c \\\\\n12c &= 2_(―) \\cdot 2_(―) \\cdot 3 \\cdot c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take one factor from each pair and multiply.\n\n$$\n\\textrm{GCF}(8a, 4c, 12c) = 2 \\cdot 2 = 4\n$$" 13194139535086 17592186045417 false] [17592186054489 1074 "To find the GCF, we list the factors of the coefficients and identify any common variable factors in the expressions.\n\nThe factors of $9$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\quad 3,\\quad 9.\n$$\n\nThe factors of $18$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\quad 2,\\quad 3,\\quad 6,\\quad 9,\\quad 18.\n$$\n\nThe factors of $27$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\quad 3,\\quad 9,\\quad 27.\n$$\n\nThe common factors of $9,$ $18,$ and $27$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\quad 3,\\quad 9.\n$$\n\nThe greatest of these common factors is $9.$\n\nNow we look at the variable factors. The expressions $9a,$ $18a,$ and $27a$ all contain $a.$\n\nThus, the $\\textrm{GCF}$ of $9a,$ $18a,$ and $27a$ is $9a.$" 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186054588 1071 "Find the greatest common factor of $2x$ and $10y$." 13194139535086 17592186045417 false] [17592186054588 1071 "What is $\\textrm{GCF}(10k,20)?$" 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186054588 1074 "First, we decompose each expression into its separate factors. We express each constant as a product of its prime factors, and we treat each variable in the product as a separate factor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x &= 2 \\cdot x \\\\\n10y &= 2 \\cdot 5 \\cdot y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we pair off the individual factors as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x &= 2_(―) \\cdot x \\\\\n10y &= 2_(―) \\cdot 5 \\cdot y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take one factor from each pair. Here, there is only one pair, so we just need to take that factor. There are no other factors to multiply it with.\n\n$$\n\\textrm{GCF}(2x,10y) = {\\color{red}{2}}\n$$\n\nThus, the greatest common factor of $2x$ and $10y$ is $2$." 13194139535086 17592186045417 false] [17592186054588 1074 "To find the GCF, we list the factors of the coefficients and identify any common variable factors in the expressions.\n\nThe factors of $10$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\quad 2,\\quad 5,\\quad 10.\n$$\n\nThe factors of $20$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\quad 2,\\quad 4,\\quad 5,\\quad 10,\\quad 20.\n$$\n\nThe common factors of $10$ and $20$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\quad 2,\\quad 5,\\quad 10.\n$$\n\nThe greatest of these common factors is $10.$\n\nNow we look at the variable factors. Since $20$ has no variable and $10k$ contains $k,$ there are no variable factors in common.\n\nThus, $\\textrm{GCF}(10k,20)=10.$" 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186054589 1071 "Find the $\\textrm{GCF}$ of $16a$ and $4a$." 13194139535086 17592186045417 false] [17592186054589 1071 "Find the greatest common factor of the terms $33p$ and $22p.$" 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186054589 1074 "First, we decompose each expression into its separate factors. We express each constant as a product of its prime factors, and we treat each variable in the product as a separate factor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n16a &= 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot a \\\\\n4a &= 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we pair off the individual factors as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n16a &= 2_(―) \\cdot 2_(―) \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot a_(―) \\\\\n4a &= 2_(―) \\cdot 2_(―) \\cdot a_(―)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take one factor from each pair and multiply.\n\n$$\n\\textrm{GCF}(16a, 4a) = \\underline{2} \\cdot \\underline{2} \\cdot \\underline{a} = 4a\n$$" 13194139535086 17592186045417 false] [17592186054589 1074 "To find the GCF, we list the factors of the coefficients and identify any common variable factors in the expressions.\n\nThe factors of $33$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\quad 3,\\quad 11,\\quad 33.\n$$\n\nThe factors of $22$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\quad 2,\\quad 11,\\quad 22.\n$$\n\nThe common factors of $33$ and $22$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\quad 11.\n$$\n\nThe greatest of these common factors is $11.$\n\nNow we look at the variable factors. Both $33p$ and $22p$ contain $p.$\n\nThus, the greatest common factor of $33p$ and $22p$ is $11p.$" 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186054659 1038 17592186459240 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186054687 1038 17592186459236 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186054687 1038 17592186459247 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186054687 1039 "Finding the Greatest Common Factor of a Number and a Variable Expression" 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186054687 1039 "Finding the Greatest Common Factor when it is a Positive Constant" 13194139535086 17592186045417 false] [17592186054688 1038 17592186459211 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186054688 1038 17592186459222 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186054688 1038 17592186459257 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186054688 1039 "Finding the Greatest Common Factor of Two Variable Expressions" 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186054688 1039 "Finding the Greatest Common Factor when it Contains Variables" 13194139535086 17592186045417 false] [17592186054756 1038 17592186459281 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186054756 1038 17592186459285 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186054756 1039 "Finding the Greatest Common Factor of Three Expressions" 13194139535086 17592186045417 false] [17592186054756 1039 "Finding the Greatest Common Factor of Three Variable Expressions" 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186054775 1038 17592186459268 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186054927 1043 17592186055777 13194139535086 17592186045417 false] [17592186054927 1050 1140.0 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186055656 1061 2779 13194139535086 17592186045417 false] [17592186055656 1061 629467 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186055657 1061 2780 13194139535086 17592186045417 false] [17592186055657 1061 629468 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186055658 1058 17592186055763 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186055658 1058 17592186055777 13194139535086 17592186045417 false] [17592186055658 1061 2784 13194139535086 17592186045417 false] [17592186055658 1061 629471 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186055763 1061 4767 13194139535086 17592186045417 false] [17592186055763 1061 629474 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186055777 1058 17592186055763 13194139535086 17592186045417 false] [17592186055936 1064 "The **greatest common factor** (or ![](images/86/861891fd828ee3558330774b23f5dcee69976d8f302da6f1e6825bf3b4be47e6.svg)) of ![](images/f5/f5a17e5e4e88d2fd8eed0ac09f0e02d77507121fac42d5f696cf407aaab6185c.svg) and ![](images/2f/2fd89ffbc3140e9c2f12576c1dec2522d603c45e8b1d4128c02892f651c6e6bb.svg) is the largest expression that will divide *both* ![](images/f5/f5a17e5e4e88d2fd8eed0ac09f0e02d77507121fac42d5f696cf407aaab6185c.svg) and ![](images/2f/2fd89ffbc3140e9c2f12576c1dec2522d603c45e8b1d4128c02892f651c6e6bb.svg) with no remainder.\n\nTo find the GCF, we list the factors of the coefficients and then identify any variable factors the expressions have in common.\n\nThe factors of ![](images/f5/f5a17e5e4e88d2fd8eed0ac09f0e02d77507121fac42d5f696cf407aaab6185c.svg) are\n![](images/05/05dbff97b169f70d1dd03beb2c23670b48520c5d98a839d218db50a06518fbf6.svg)\n\nThe factors of ![](images/34/34d666f013d8ce4f2b50c0a9ab9c2b5808feb37163ba38470555867babada4ef.svg) are\n![](images/59/59919e2659e3bb232e4dbc1857c9f0eca27706bbad72aea6a55bb749706307a2.svg)\n\nThe common factors of ![](images/f5/f5a17e5e4e88d2fd8eed0ac09f0e02d77507121fac42d5f696cf407aaab6185c.svg) and ![](images/34/34d666f013d8ce4f2b50c0a9ab9c2b5808feb37163ba38470555867babada4ef.svg) are\n![](images/b0/b08502484874a42582d63ddfc0eda0048576d0cc460aced250ad962721d0e985.svg)\n\nThe greatest of these common factors is ![](images/fe/fe34f715005da1a8b682c9650a76785673fdf25c3b7c7546bb94824470c54caf.svg)\n\nNow we look at the variable factors. Since ![](images/f5/f5a17e5e4e88d2fd8eed0ac09f0e02d77507121fac42d5f696cf407aaab6185c.svg) has no variable and ![](images/2f/2fd89ffbc3140e9c2f12576c1dec2522d603c45e8b1d4128c02892f651c6e6bb.svg) contains ![](images/5a/5aac2c81021f3f9e468e7840c5f95ece35c10b894bdae19ec1d247bdeb47ac6f.svg) there are no variable factors in common.\n\nThus, the greatest common factor of ![](images/f5/f5a17e5e4e88d2fd8eed0ac09f0e02d77507121fac42d5f696cf407aaab6185c.svg) and ![](images/2f/2fd89ffbc3140e9c2f12576c1dec2522d603c45e8b1d4128c02892f651c6e6bb.svg) is ![](images/fe/fe34f715005da1a8b682c9650a76785673fdf25c3b7c7546bb94824470c54caf.svg)" 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186055936 1064 "The **greatest common factor** (or $\\textrm{GCF}$) of $12$ and $18x$ is the largest expression that will divide *both* $12$ and $18x$ with no remainder.\n\nTo find the GCF, we first decompose each expression into its separate factors. We express each constant as a product of its prime factors, and we treat each variable in the product as a separate factor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12 &= 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n18x &= 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we pair off the individual factors as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12 &= 2_(―) \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3_(―) \\\\\n18x &= 2_(―) \\cdot 3_(―) \\cdot 3 \\cdot x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take one factor from each pair and multiply.\n\n$$\n\\textrm{GCF}(12,18x) = {\\color{red}2} \\cdot {\\color{blue}3} = 6\n$$\n\nThus, the greatest common factor of $12$ and $18x$ is $6$." 13194139535086 17592186045417 false] [17592186064328 1038 17592186459295 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186064341 1038 17592186459231 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186068679 1038 17592186459302 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186078472 1038 17592186459272 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186078531 1038 17592186459300 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186087875 1038 17592186459289 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186088223 1068 17592186085992 13194139535086 17592186045417 false] [17592186088223 1074 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +4(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-12(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=12{x}^{2}-32x-30\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n-12\\alpha {x}^{2}+(8\\alpha -12\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +4\\beta -12\\gamma )=12{x}^{2}-32x-30\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-12\\alpha =12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 8\\alpha -12\\beta =-32\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +4\\beta -12\\gamma =-30\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\beta =2,\\gamma =3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}+2x+3.\n$$" 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186088223 1074 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +4(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-12(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=12{x}^{2}-32x-30\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n-12\\alpha {x}^{2}+(8\\alpha -12\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +4\\beta -12\\gamma )=12{x}^{2}-32x-30\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-12\\alpha =12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 8\\alpha -12\\beta =-32\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +4\\beta -12\\gamma =-30\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\beta =2,\\gamma =3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}+2x+3.\n$$" 13194139535086 17592186045417 false] [17592186098935 1078 17592186098936 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186101021 1038 17592186459215 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186101021 1038 17592186459232 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186101021 1038 17592186459242 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186101021 1038 17592186459273 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186101021 1038 17592186459274 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186101022 1038 17592186459207 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186101022 1038 17592186459219 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186101022 1038 17592186459253 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186101023 1038 17592186459230 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186101023 1038 17592186459251 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186101023 1038 17592186459276 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186101023 1038 17592186459304 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186101024 1038 17592186459216 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186101024 1038 17592186459287 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186101024 1038 17592186459291 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186101060 1050 1380.0 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186101269 1070 17592186045485 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186101269 1071 "![](images/d6/d661fd02727800f40ccaaf7f388b99b61b9e3a2d6c2da351c47b2166d1182b48.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $f(x)$, which is defined on $[- 3, 6]$, is shown above. The areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $40$, $20$ and $10$, respectively (from left to right). Find the average value of $f(x)$ on $[- 3, 6]$." 13194139535086 17592186045417 false] [17592186101269 1071 "![](images/d6/d661fd02727800f40ccaaf7f388b99b61b9e3a2d6c2da351c47b2166d1182b48.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $f(x)$, which is defined on $[-3,6]$, is shown above. The areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $40$, $20$ and $10,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the average value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,6].$" 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186101269 1074 "The average value of a function on a given interval is\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, we see that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=40-20+10=30.\n$$\n\nSo, applying the average value formula, we find that the average value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6-(-3)}\\cdot (30) \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186101908 1061 618889 13194139535086 17592186045417 false] [17592186101908 1061 618980 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186101909 1061 618890 13194139535086 17592186045417 false] [17592186101909 1061 618981 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186101910 1061 618894 13194139535086 17592186045417 false] [17592186101910 1061 618984 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186101911 1061 618895 13194139535086 17592186045417 false] [17592186101911 1061 618985 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186101912 1061 618898 13194139535086 17592186045417 false] [17592186101912 1061 618989 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186101913 1061 618901 13194139535086 17592186045417 false] [17592186101913 1061 618992 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186102034 1064 "The region $\\textrm{R},$ shown below, is the area under the graph of $f(x) = 2x+1$ over the interval $[1,3].$ What is the height of the rectangle defined over the same interval that has the same area as $\\textrm{R}?$ \n\n![](images/01/01597200b1f0e147fa8c53b6301d0c8e37208b5a6cce64cf0d9859d1220d8385.png)\n\n \n\nUsing the formula for the area of a trapezoid, we have that\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\textrm{Area}\\,(\\textrm{R}) = \\dfrac{1}{2}(3+7)(3-1)= 10.}\n$$\n\nFor the rectangle to have the same area, we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =f(c)(3-1) \\\\ f(c) & =5 \\\\ 2c+1 & =5 \\\\ c & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can summarize what we've just done by saying that\n\n$$\n\\int_1^3 (2x +1)\\, \\textrm d x= f(2) \\cdot (3-1).\n$$\n\nThis is an example of the **mean value theorem for integrals**, which states:\n\n*If $f(x)$ is a continuous function on the closed interval $[a, b]$, then there exists a number $c$ in that interval such that*\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\, \\textrm d x = f(c) \\cdot (b-a).\n$$" 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186102034 1064 "The region $\\textrm{R},$ shown below, is the area under the graph of $f(x) = 2x+1$ over the interval $[1,3].$ What is the height of the rectangle defined over the same interval that has the same area as $\\textrm{R}?$ \n\n![](images/01/01597200b1f0e147fa8c53b6301d0c8e37208b5a6cce64cf0d9859d1220d8385.png)\n\nUsing the formula for the area of a trapezoid, we have that\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\textrm{Area}\\,(\\textrm{R}) = \\dfrac{1}{2}(3+7)(3-1)= 10.}\n$$\n\nFor the rectangle to have the same area, we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =f(c)(3-1) \\\\ f(c) & =5 \\\\ 2c+1 & =5 \\\\ c & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can summarize what we've just done by saying that\n\n$$\n\\int_1^3 (2x +1)\\, \\textrm d x= f(2) \\cdot (3-1).\n$$\n\nThis is an example of the **mean value theorem for integrals**, which states:\n\n> *If $f(x)$ is a continuous function on the closed interval $[a, b]$, then there exists a number $c$ in that interval such that*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\int_a^b f(x)\\, \\textrm d x = f(c) \\cdot (b-a).\n> $$" 13194139535086 17592186045417 false] [17592186105907 1038 17592186459261 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186105907 1038 17592186459275 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186105907 1038 17592186459283 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186105907 1038 17592186459306 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186105908 1038 17592186459221 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186105908 1038 17592186459264 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [17592186105908 1038 17592186459308 13194139535086 17592186045417 true] [1759218610